WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.790
The supplied diagram distinguishes high-temperature, low-pressure conditions;

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:08.810
high-pressure, relatively low-temperature conditions; and typical regional conditions.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:13.070
Pressure increases with burial, but temperature depends on the thermal history.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.230
High pressure does not always mean the hottest rock.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:20.190
A shallow intrusion heats adjacent country rock and creates a contact

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:24.690
aureole. That setting can reach high temperature at relatively low pressure.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:29.330
The aureole’s extent and mineral assemblages depend on heat, host composition,

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:30.010
and fluids.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:35.090
At an ocean–continent convergent boundary, a relatively cold slab descends and

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.330
experiences increasing pressure before it fully warms.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:42.790
High-pressure, low-temperature metamorphism can occur along the subduction zone;

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.510
a shallow magma body has a contrasting pressure–temperature history.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:52.510
During continent–continent collision, thrusting and crustal thickening bury and heat rocks

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:57.410
over broad regions. Regional metamorphism and deformation can accompany one another.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.910
Map the location and conditions separately rather than assigning one condition

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:02.990
to an entire plate boundary.
